@startuml vinga-conversation-turn
' One conversation turn as the code actually runs it. Ordering and
' concurrency are the point here, which is what a sequence diagram can
' show and a box-and-arrow picture cannot: the tool loop's rounds, the
' sentence lookahead overlapping playback, and who re-arms the listening.

title vinga: one conversation turn, from wake word to spoken reply

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam backgroundColor white
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam BoxPadding 12
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left

actor "Person" as H
participant "ESP32-S3\ndevice" as D #EEF3FF

box "vinga-server" #EEF8EE
  participant "Session" as S
  participant "Endpointer\n(silero)" as V
  participant "ASR" as A
  participant "LLM" as L
  participant "Tools" as T
  participant "TTS" as P
end box

== Before the first turn (once per boot, once per connection) ==

D -> S : POST /xiaozhi/ota/ with system info
S --> D : websocket url, device token,\nprotocol version, wall clock
note right of D
  Persisted to NVS. vinga-server serves no
  firmware images, so the firmware section
  always answers "up to date", and the reply
  never carries an activation section.
end note
D -> S : WebSocket upgrade,\nAuthorization: Bearer <token>
note over S : Token verified **before** the accept.\nA failure is an HTTP 403 on the upgrade,\nwhich stock firmware retries and refreshes.
D -> S : hello (audio params, device MAC, client id)
S --> D : hello (opus, 24 kHz, 60 ms frames)
note over S : Ten seconds each way, then the session gives up.\nThe device is resolved to an agent here; a device\nbound to no agent is told so and closed.
S ->> D : mcp initialize, tools/list
D -->> S : self.* tools (volume, brightness, screen)
note right of S : Discovery runs as a background task,\nso a first utterance never waits on it.

== Flow 1: from the voice to the model ==

H -> D : wake word (ESP-SR on the chip),\nor a button press
D -> S : listen start (mode: auto | manual | realtime)
H -> D : speaks
note over D
  The speaker and the microphone sit centimetres
  apart, so the microphone hears the assistant too.
  Hardware echo cancellation (ES7210 + ES8311)
  subtracts the known playback before anything
  else runs. It is what makes barge-in possible.
end note
D -> S : binary Opus frames, 16 kHz, 60 ms each\n(v1 bare packets; v2 and v3 prefix a header)
S -> S : decode to PCM, s16le mono 16 kHz

loop while the device is listening
  S -> V : feed(pcm)
  V --> S : still speaking
end
V --> S : **utterance ended**\n(enough trailing silence)

S -> S : trim to speech_start minus\nutterance_pre_roll_ms (300 ms default)
note right of S
  A continuously listening device piles silence in
  front of every utterance. Only the endpointer knows
  where the speech began, so it reports the offset and
  the session cuts there rather than transcribing the
  wait. The buffer is bounded: a realtime session
  listens through the silences for the whole session.
end note

S -> A : transcribe(pcm, 16000, language_hint)
A --> S : text, detected language,\noptional lock_language
note right of A
  `lock_language` is the provider asking the session
  to reuse a language for the rest of the session.
  It is how a per-session policy lives in a provider
  that is shared between sessions and holds no
  per-session state of its own.
end note
S -> D : stt (the transcript, for the display)

== The reply: a tool loop the session owns ==

note over S, T
  The loop lives in the session, not in a provider,
  because only the session can change agents between
  rounds. Up to MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS = 4; the last round is
  sent with tool_choice = "none", so a reply always
  ends in speech rather than in a tool nobody hears.
end note

loop up to 4 rounds
  S -> L : stream(system prompt + memory,\nturns, tool snapshot, tool_choice)
  L --> S : text deltas, or tool calls
  alt the model asked for tools
    S -> T : execute (builtin, self_*, or <entry>__*)
    T --> S : results, failures included
    note right of T : A failure is a result too: the model\nphrases what to tell the user, in its\nown voice and the user's language.
  else the model is speaking
  end
end

== Flow 2: from the model's words to the ears ==

S -> S : SentenceSplitter: cut on sentence-ending\npunctuation once the whitespace has arrived
S -> D : tts start

loop per sentence
  S -> P : synthesize(sentence)
  activate P
  P --> S : PCM chunks at the voice's rate
  S -> S : resample to 24 kHz, encode 60 ms Opus,\npace to realtime
  S -> D : tts sentence_start + Opus frames
  D -> H : speaks the sentence
  S -> P : synthesize(**next** sentence) while this one plays
  deactivate P
end

note over S, P
  Synthesizing only after a sentence finished playing puts
  its whole time to first byte on the speaker as silence,
  once per sentence: measured at 617 ms and 520 ms on a
  three-sentence reply, and reported from a board session
  as "hiccups in the assistant's voice". Running ahead
  spends that time against playback already happening.
  The lookahead buffer holds **one chunk**, not the sentence,
  so the paced consumer keeps its backpressure on a provider
  that can produce far faster than realtime.
end note

S -> D : tts stop

alt auto mode
  D -> S : listen start (the device re-arms itself)
else realtime mode
  note over D, S : The device asked once and never stopped streaming,\nso the session never stops listening. It therefore\nhears the user through its own reply, which is\nwhat makes barge-in reachable (and gated).
else manual mode
  H -> D : press to talk again
end

note over S : Silence past server.limits.idle_timeout_s\n(120 s by default) hangs up a realtime session,\nwhich would otherwise hold a slot forever.

@enduml
